# Both api and connect derive every client's address from the ingress, and
# getting it wrong is not a cosmetic problem. connect turns that address into
# the client's location: a private address has no mmdb entry, so
# GuessLocationType returns "Unknown location type", no network_client_location
# row is written, no provider reaches network_client_location_reliability, and
# the provider location list silently empties. api feeds the same address to
# rate limiting and the ARIN foreign-provider score, so one shared address
# means the entire deployment shares a single signup budget.
#
# The contract is now a single header, set by the ingress -- see the
# (client_address) snippet below.
(client_address) {
	# As of the 2026-09-10 upstream merge, session.ResolveClientAddress reads
	# exactly ONE header -- X-UR-Forwarded-For -- and deliberately ignores
	# X-Forwarded-For and X-Forwarded-Source-Port. The ingress is required to
	# overwrite it with one ip:port pair from the socket it accepted, which is
	# the same contract the upstream nginx ingress implements
	# (controller/verify_controller.go:139).
	#
	# header_up OVERWRITES rather than appends, and that is what makes this
	# safe: a client that sends its own X-UR-Forwarded-For has the value
	# replaced with the address Caddy actually accepted the connection from, so
	# it cannot forge a location or evade the per-address rate limits.
	#
	# Do NOT go back to stripping this header. Stripping left the api with no
	# forwarded address at all, so every client fell back to Caddy's bridge
	# address, the whole deployment shared one signup budget, and real users
	# got 429/503 on their first account.
	header_up X-UR-Forwarded-For "{http.request.remote}"
}

api.beta-test.net {
	reverse_proxy api:8080 {
		import client_address
	}
}

connect.beta-test.net {
	reverse_proxy connect:80 {
		import client_address
	}
}

# proxy.beta-test.net exists here only so caddy obtains and renews a Let's
# Encrypt certificate for it. Caddy does NOT carry proxy traffic: the proxy
# server terminates its own TLS on its own ports (see the `proxy` service),
# reading the cert from the vault at tls/proxy.beta-test.net/. This block is
# what makes the ACME HTTP-01 challenge on :80 succeed.
proxy.beta-test.net {
	respond "urnetwork proxy" 200
}
